speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, some questions were asked which I should like to answer at this
Supply-National Defence time. The date of the naval order with regard to Canada badges, asked for by the hon. member for Three Rivers, was May 13, 1946.
The number of privates in the Canadian army (reserve) at October 31, 1949, is approximately 24,000. I say "approximately" because a return was requested from local commands on October 31, 1949, for this information. The information is collected at local commands, and had to be verified from certain units. We have received final returns from the areas representing 70 per cent of the strength, and extending the figures from the areas definitely reporting so as to cover the whole, gives a figure of approximately 24,000. That would be correct within 100.
The hon. member for Nanaimo asked questions with regard to air crew. In Canada at the present time there are approximately
60,000	men now in civilian life who during the second world war served as pilots, navigators, wireless air gunners and bombardiers. Approximately 47,000 have signified their intention of joining the armed forces again should a state of emergency arise. Because of age and physical factors the percentage of those suitable for employment in air crew duties will be reduced progressively each year.
In the R.C.A.F. itself, out of a strength of 16,356 there are 1,630 who are qualified air crew in the regular forces. Out of 3,086 in the air force auxiliary 471 are air crew. During the fiscal year 1949-50, it is estimated, approximately 200 men will qualify as air crew. There are now seven channels through which men may be qualified for air crew:
1.	Men entering the R.C.A.F. from civil life for long service commands;
2.	Men entering the R.C.A.F. from civil life for short service commissions;
3.	University undergraduates who, as flying cadets, qualify as air crew throughout full-time work during the summer;
4.	Cadets at Royal Roads and R.M.C. who will similarly qualify as flying cadets;
5.	Serving officers in air crew trades which have been abolished-gunners, and flight engineers-who are being retrained as pilots, radio officers or navigators;
6.	Serving airmen who have applied and have been selected for air crew training; and
7.	Civilians with previous R.C.A.F. experience either as officers or airmen.
That covers all the classes by which men may be qualified.
